    "gap_after": 0.07,
    "level_db": -66.6
   },
   "adjustments": [],
   "text": "The biggest mistake I see is staying awake all day after the last night shift. Thinking you'll reset by powering through. What actually happens is you push the circadian clock even further out of phase. So the next night you're exhausted before you even start. The gentler approach call it a half -flip. Is to sleep for a short -core block. Say 3 hours right after the shift, then stay up for a few hours, get bright light. And finally go to bed at a reasonable evening hour. This splits the adjustment into two smaller jumps instead of one massive leap. And most people report feeling functional within 48 hours, instead of a week. So the half -flip gives the clock a manageable step."
  }
